  ],
  "hints": [
    "The only thing that survives from one day to the next is whether you are currently holding a share, so describe the state by prefix of days rather than by the individual transactions you have made.",
    "Write dp[t] as the best profit from the first t days with everything sold off, and ask which purchase day j the sale on day t - 1 could belong to, adding dp[j + 1] for the days before that purchase.",
    "You never need the whole table. Two days at a time are enough: a rise from day i to day i + 1 can be collected on its own, because selling and rebuying on the same day changes nothing, so the answer is the sum of all positive consecutive price differences."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force is a dynamic program over prefixes. Let dp[t] be the best profit obtainable from the first t days with every position already closed, so dp[0] = 0 and dp[1] = 0. The only new event on day t - 1 is a sale, and the share being sold was bought on some day j with j + 1 < t, which leaves dp[j + 1] as the best plan for the days before that purchase. Trying every j gives dp[t] = max(dp[t - 1], max over j of dp[j + 1] + prices[t - 1] - prices[j]), which is O(n\u00b2) time and O(n) space. The optimal solution notices that profit can be split anywhere: selling on a long rise earns the same as selling after every single day of that rise, because the intermediate sells are immediately rebought. The total therefore equals the sum of every positive difference prices[i] - prices[i - 1], computed in one pass with one accumulator.",
    "why_optimal": "One pass over the array reads each price exactly once and keeps a single running total, so the optimal solution uses O(n) time and O(1) extra space. The O(n) bound is the floor here: an untouched price could be the peak that changes the answer, so every element has to be inspected at least once, and the greedy pass reads nothing twice. The quadratic version is slower only because of its inner scan over candidate buy days; its recurrence restricts itself to at most one open transaction and to sells on later days, so it explores exactly the same feasible set of transactions and returns the same number.",
    "pitfalls": "Carrying a running minimum across the whole array and forgetting to restart it after selling, which quietly collapses the problem into the single-transaction version and undercounts on rising prices. Initialising the best profit to a negative sentinel, which reports a loss for prices that only fall where 0 is correct. Letting the inner loop start at j + 1 = t, which would pair a day with itself and add a phantom zero trade while missing the last day's sale when t is small. A single-element array must return 0, and equal consecutive prices contribute nothing."
  },
